TWO DECLARATION LINES ACCOUNTED FOR 11% OF THE STRICT SURFACE.

All 105 mutable-override errors carried a byte-identical message naming
the same base class: 105 analyzer subclasses across all three language
packages each narrowed TreeSitterAnalyzer.grammar_module or
.language_pack_name from `str | None` to `str`, which mypy reports as a
covariant override of a MUTABLE attribute. One error per file, 33 in
mainstream + 39 in extended1 + 33 in common.

The error is reported at each SUBCLASS while the defect lives in the
BASE, so reading any individual one tells you nothing; it only resolves
on noticing that all 105 messages are identical. Both attributes are now
read-only properties returning `str | None`. Zero subclass edits. An
attribute that is only ever read stops claiming to be mutable.

RUNTIME-NEUTRAL BY MRO, VERIFIED RATHER THAN ARGUED. A subclass
class-body assignment precedes this base in the MRO, so
`self.grammar_module` still returns the string; a subclass declaring
neither falls through to the property and gets None. Checked live on
real analyzers: CppAnalyzer -> 'tree_sitter_cpp', IniTreeSitterAnalyzer
-> language-pack 'ini', base -> None for both. 16446 tests pass; the
change touches the base class of every tree-sitter analyzer in the tool
and _check_grammar_available / _create_parser both branch on these
values, so a silent grammar-disabling regression was the risk under test.

THE TEMPTING FIX WOULD HAVE BROKEN 60 ANALYZERS. `ClassVar[str] = ""` is
tidier and matches the lang / pass_id sentinels a few lines above -- and
it is a live behaviour change: five `is not None` checks become
permanently true, so every language-pack analyzer would call
importlib.import_module(""); and _get_config_dict feeds
compute_config_fingerprint, a PERSISTED cache key, so every analyzer's
fingerprint would change. ClassVar alone does not silence the error, and
Final makes it worse (adds "cannot assign to final name" at all 105
sites). Residual sharp edges of the property form, both grepped before
committing: a CLASS-level read now yields the property object rather
than None, and `self.grammar_module = ...` would raise -- every read in
packages/*/src/ is self.-qualified and there are zero such assignments
anywhere in the repo.

Also in this commit: 20 type-arg errors in ir.py and analyze/base.py,
types read off the construction site rather than the name.
global_symbols is dict[str, Symbol] because register_symbol does
`global_symbols[symbol.name] = symbol`; the occurrence-counter key tuple
leads with `str | None` because protocol_origin is declared Optional.
Both are CORE modules and both revealed zero hidden errors -- which
refutes the centrality hypothesis the earlier batches suggested. The
predictor of cascade risk is interface polymorphism, not centrality.

